<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achyuta Deva Raya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1529">1529</a>–<a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achyuta Deva Raya was emperor of Vijayanagara from 1529 to 1542, succeeding his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a>. He kept most of the empire's territory, campaigning in the south and recovering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, but faced growing rivalry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aliya-rama-raya">Aliya Rama Raya</a> and other nobles. The Achyutaraya temple at Hampi is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achyuta-deva-raya.jpg" alt="Achyuta Deva Raya" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ravitheja Kumar Reddy C · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achyutadevaraya_and_his_consort_Varadarajamma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
